  "query_preview": "..High pressure will slowly and grudgingly retreat \neastward over the next several days, as a long-wave trough forms \nover the eastern Pacific. In fact, much of the next 10 days looks \nlike an ongoing battle between the high pressure to our east and \nthe troughing to our northwest.\n\nWhat does this do? Well, in the short term, it allows capping to \nweaken and southerly flow to increase across the region. As long as \nthe moisture remains in place, this is a very good recipe for active \nweather. Today looks like the most active day, with slight downturns \nand a eastward shift in activity afterwards. We expect moderate to \nhigh grade Monsoon conditions today as a result and a Flash Flood \nWatch has been issued.\n\nWhy has a Flash Flood Watch been issued? Many locations are quite \nsaturated at this point and when combined with increased instability \nvalues, shear and PW values over the 90th percentile, the chances \nfor flooding are greatly increased.\n\nBy the end of the week and into next weekend, there is potential for \nsome drying, as the west coast trough attempts to move inland. This \ncould bring drier, mid-level air and effectively reduce convection \nover our state. However, if the trough does not move inland as much \nor if it ejects farther north, it may just keep our active weather \ngoing. For now, we have tapered off PoPs a little bit, but we will \nneed to watch this pattern closely, as any change on the trough \nposition could make a big difference.\n\nTemperatures will continue to trend down with the cloud cover and \nprecipitation, heat related headlines will not be needed.",
